# Why `strip_tags` Isn't Enough for Pure Text Cleaning

When you need to sanitize user input to allow only alphanumeric characters and whitespace, relying solely on PHP's `strip_tags()` function is a common misstep. While this function effectively removes HTML tags, it leaves behind non-breaking entities like `&nbsp;`, special symbols such as `&quot;`, and other encoded characters that can still break your application logic or database constraints.

Consider the scenario where you are processing a string like `"Hello world)&lt;b&gt; (*&amp;^%$#@! it's me: and; love you.&lt;p&gt;"`. If you run `strip_tags()` on this, you get `"Hello world) ( *&amp;^%$#@! it's me: and; love you."`. The tags are gone, but the punctuation and special symbols remain. To achieve a strict whitelist of only letters (A-Z, a-z), numbers (0-9), and spaces, you need a more aggressive sanitization strategy.

## The Regex Approach for Strict Whitelisting

The most efficient way to strip everything except your desired characters is using regular expressions. PHP's `preg_replace()` function allows you to define a pattern that matches unwanted characters and replaces them with an empty string.

To keep only alphanumeric characters and whitespace, you can use the following logic:

```php
$input = "Hello world)&lt;b&gt; (*&amp;^%$#@! it's me: and; love you.&lt;p&gt;";

// Define a pattern that matches anything NOT in your whitelist (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, space)
// The negated character class [^\w\s] handles this. 
// \w includes underscores by default, so we must exclude them explicitly if needed.
$clean = preg_replace('/[^\w\s]/u', '', $input);

// If you strictly want to remove underscores as well:
$strict_clean = preg_replace('/[^\p{L}\p{N}\s]/u', '', $clean);

echo $strict_clean; 
// Output: Hello world it s me and love you
```

In this example, the first regex removes HTML entities and special symbols but keeps underscores because `\w` includes them. If your requirement is strictly A-Z, a-z, 0-9, and whitespace, the second pattern using `\p{L}` (any Unicode letter) and `\p{N}` (any Unicode number) provides better control, though for standard ASCII-only needs, a simple negated character class works fine.

## Handling Non-Breaking Spaces and Entities

A frequent pitfall when cleaning text is that `strip_tags()` does not decode HTML entities. If the input contains `&nbsp;` or `&quot;`, these remain as literal strings after stripping tags unless you explicitly decode them first.

You should always call `html_entity_decode()` before applying your custom filtering logic. This ensures that encoded characters like `&amp;` (which represents an ampersand) are converted back to their actual symbols, which your regex can then identify and remove.

```php
$input = "Hello &nbsp; world &quot;&lt;b&gt;";
$decoded = html_entity_decode($input); 
// Result: Hello   world "<b>
$clean = preg_replace('/[^\p{L}\p{N}\s]/u', '', $decoded);
```

This two-step processâdecoding first, then filteringâis essential for robust data cleaning pipelines. Skipping the decoding step often leads to hidden characters persisting in your output, which can cause issues when storing data in strict database fields or sending it to APIs that reject special characters.

## Performance and Encoding Considerations

When implementing these solutions in high-traffic applications, performance matters. Regular expressions are generally fast for simple character removal, but the overhead of Unicode property escapes (`\p{L}`, `\p{N}`) can be slightly higher than ASCII-only patterns if you don't need international support.

If your application handles only standard English text, a simpler ASCII-based regex like `/[^\w\s]/` might suffice, provided you manually remove underscores afterward or adjust the character class to `[A-Za-z0-9\s]`. Always ensure your `mb_internal_encoding()` is set correctly if dealing with non-ASCII content, as incorrect encoding assumptions can cause regex patterns to behave unexpectedly.

For large-scale data processing, consider caching the cleaned results if the input strings are repeated, rather than running the regex every time. Additionally, be wary of using this logic on untrusted input without prior validation; combining `strip_tags()` with a strict character whitelist is often safer than relying on whitelisting alone, as it handles edge cases where malicious payloads attempt to bypass filters through encoded entities.
